L000040	\affiliation{Center for Computation and Technology, Louisiana State University, Baton Rouge, Louisiana 70803, USA}
L000041	
L000042	\begin{abstract}
L000043	We investigate universal features of measurement-and-feedback control of quantum chaotic dynamics by examining the quantum Arnold cat map, a paradigmatic model of quantum chaos.
L000044	Inspired by probabilistic control of classical chaos, our protocol stochastically alternates between intrinsic instability and engineered control operations that steer trajectories toward a target point.
L000045	Simulation of exact quantum dynamics and a semiclassical truncated Wigner approximation reveal universal properties of the cat map's control transition.
L000046	To further characterize this universality, we introduce the inverted harmonic oscillator as an analytically tractable effective model of instability.
L000047	By integrating numerical simulations, a semiclassical Fokker-Planck description, and a direct spectral analysis of the stochastic quantum channel, we identify quantum signatures absent in classical limits.
L000048	The close agreement between quantum simulation, truncated Wigner approximation, and inverted oscillator analysis shows that universal features of the transition are set by uncertainty-limited quantum fluctuations and are insensitive to genuine quantum interference.
L000049	\end{abstract}
L000050	
L000051	\maketitle
L000052	
L000053	\textit{Introduction}---Order emerges from chaotic dynamics across a range of scales, from microscopic quantum systems to macroscopic classical, and even socio-economic, processes.
L000054	In many-body systems, local interactions are sufficient to induce ordering.
L000055	Bird flocks and fish schools provide familiar classical examples~\cite{vicsek1995novel, toner1995long}, superconductivity and superfluidity represent analogous quantum phenomena, and within statistical mechanics, ordered states emerge through phase transitions.
L000056	
L000057	Beyond passive emergence, order can be engineered from chaotic dynamics using both deterministic procedures, e.g. the Ott–Grebogi–Yorke method~\cite{Ott1990}, and probabilistic approaches, involving the stochastic intervention of a control map with probability $p$ into chaotic dynamics~\cite{Antoniou1996,Antoniou1997,Antoniou1998}.
L000058	In this scheme the control map and chaotic dynamics share a periodic orbit, unstable for the latter and stable for the former, which becomes a global attractor above a critical control rate $p_c$.
L000059	This is a random multiplicative process where distance from the control point is either amplified by a factor $e^{\kappa}$ by chaotic dynamics or contracted by a factor $e^{-\gamma}$ by control; this has been studied in turbulent systems \cite{Friedrich1997,Naert1997,Benzi2003,Durrive2020} and market dynamics \cite{Levy1994,Levy1995,Levy1996,Frisch1997,Laherrere1998,Takayasu2000,Friedrich2000,Sornette2001}.
L000060	These ideas have also recently gained resonance in quantum systems, where measurement-conditioned feedback operations can stabilize states and steer dynamics~\cite{Iadecola2023,Herasymenko2023,Buchhold2022,Ravindranath2022,ODea2022,Friedman2022,Piroli2023,Sierant2022,Sierant2023,LeMaire2024,Milekhin2024,Allocca2024,Ravindranath2025,Gerbino2025}.
L000061	
L000062	In this Letter, we study stochastic measurement-and-feedback control of quantized chaotic maps, focusing on the quantum Arnold cat map~\cite{Arnold1968}.
L000063	Using exact quantum dynamics and the truncated-Wigner-approximation (TWA), we extract universal properties of the quantum control transition.
L000064	Since essential features of the transition are determined by the local dynamics near the control point, it can be effectively described by an inverted harmonic oscillator (IHO)\footnote{The IHO arises broadly across physics \cite{Barton1986,Gentilini2015,Betzios2016,Dalui2019,Hegde2019,Subramanyan2021}.}.
L000065	Focusing on this model, we use numerical simulations of Gaussian-state evolution, a semiclassical Fokker-Planck (FP) analysis augmented by quantum uncertainty, and exact eigenoperators of the stochastic quantum channel, to characterize the transition and the controlled phase.
L000066	
L000067	Despite lacking a compact phase space, genuine quantum chaos, or quantum interference, the IHO shows excellent agreement with controlled cat-map dynamics.
L000068	This indicates that the essential physics of \emph{quantum} stochastic control is governed locally near the unstable fixed point.
L000069	The measurement-and-feedback protocol suppresses wave-packet spreading and self-interference, effectively pushing the Ehrenfest time to infinity and rendering interference largely irrelevant for controllability.
L000070	
L000071	
L000072	\textit{Classical Control}---We begin with a brief illustration of classical control of chaos.
L000073	Take $\mathbf{r}(t)$ to be the small displacement from an unstable fixed point or periodic orbit in a 2d phase space, where $t$ is a discrete time index.
L000074	There is always a choice of coordinates where chaotic and control maps act on $\mathbf{r}(t)$ as $\mathcal{S}=\mathrm{diag}(e^\kappa,e^{-\kappa})$ and $\mathcal{C}=\mathrm{diag}(e^{-\gamma},e^{-\gamma})$ respectively, with $\kappa,\gamma>0$ parameterizing their respective strengths ($\det \mathcal{S} = 1$ due to being area-preserving).
L000075	The behavior of the first component $r_1$ determines controllability of the system: chaos is controlled when the Lyapunov exponent $\overline{\ln\abs{r_1(t)}}<0$ as $t\to\infty$, where $\overline{(\cdots)}$ represents averaging over stochastic trajectories.
L000076	The average of this quantity can be performed analytically, resulting in a critical control rate \cite{Antoniou1998,KTinprep},
L000077	\begin{equation} \label{eq:pc}
L000078	    p_c = \frac{\kappa}{\kappa+\gamma},
L000079	\end{equation}
L000080	above which the system is controlled, and below which chaos prevails.
L000081	
L000082	
L000083	\textit{Quantized Chaotic Map}---We use the Arnold cat map~\cite{Arnold1968} as a paradigmatic example of 2d chaos.
L000084	Classically, this map transforms the unit square as
L000085	\begin{equation}
L000086	    \mathbf{r}(t+1) = \begin{pmatrix}
L000087	        2 & 1 \\
L000088	        1 & 1 \\
L000089	    \end{pmatrix}\mathbf{r}(t) \mod 1,
L000090	\end{equation}
L000091	and has fixed point $\mathbf{r}_0 = 0$ and strength $\kappa = 2\ln(\frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2})$.
L000092	To quantize this map we promote the two coordinates to non-commuting quantum operators: $\mathbf{r}=(r_1\,\,\, r_2)\to\hat{\mathbf{r}}=(\hat{x}\,\,\,\hat{p})$, with $[\hat{x},\hat{p}] = i\hbar$.
L000093	The cat map can be implemented as unitary evolution on these operators with $\hat{U}_\mathrm{cat} = e^{-i\hat{p}^2/(2\hbar)}e^{i\hat{x}^2/(2\hbar)}$.
L000094	We take discrete position eigenstates $\ket{x_n=n/N}$ and momentum eigenstates $\ket{p_m=m/N}$, $n,m=0,1,\dots,N-1$.
L000095	With $\ket{x_n} = \frac{1}{N}\sum_m e^{2\pi i nm/N}\ket{p_m}$ and unit-square phase space, $\hbar=\frac{1}{2\pi N}$~\cite{Supplement,Hannay1980}.
L000096	The continuum limit $N\to\infty$ is therefore the classical limit.
L000097	
L000098	Because unitary quantizations arise from area-preserving maps~\cite{ArnoldWeinstein1989,DeBievreDegliEsposti1998}, implementing non-area-preserving control requires measurement.
L000099	We do this with a positive operator-valued measure (POVM) defined by Kraus operators.
L000100	To construct them, we introduce a basis via $[2 - \cos(2\pi \hat x) - \cos(2\pi \hat p)]\ket{n_c} = \epsilon_{n_c}\ket{n_c}$ such that $\ket{0}$ is the controlled state (fixing the phases of the basis by the harmonic oscillator convention, see \cite{Supplement}).
L000101	This allows us to construct Kraus operators
L000102	\begin{equation} \label{eq:ctrlKraus}
L000103	    \hat K_m(\theta) = \frac{(-i)^m}{\sqrt{m!}}\sin^m(\theta) (\cos(\theta))^{\hat{a}^\dagger \hat{a}} \hat{a}^m,
L000104	\end{equation}
L000105	where $\theta\in[0,\pi/2]$ parametrizes the strength of control, $\hat a = \sum_{n_c=0}^{N_\mathrm{ctrl}-1} \sqrt{n_c} \ket{n_c-1}\bra{n_c}$ where we take $N_\mathrm{ctrl} = N/2$, and complete the POVM with a projector on the remaining basis states.
L000106	This control channel acts on $\hat{x}$ and $\hat{p}$ as a rescaling by $\cos\theta$, so classical and quantum control strengths $\gamma$ and $\theta$ are related as $\cos\theta=e^{-\gamma}$.
L000107	
L000108	Both quantum channels (cat map and control) can be simulated with the truncated Wigner approximation (TWA) \cite{Polkovnikov2010}.
L000109	In the TWA, dynamics are classical with quantum fluctuations sampled from the initial Wigner function $W_0(x,p) = \frac1{\pi \hbar} e^{-(x^2+p^2)/\hbar}$, while control induces noise $(x,p)\mapsto \cos\theta(x,p)+\bm{\eta}$ with $\bm{\eta}$ Gaussian of variance $\frac{\hbar}{2}(1-e^{-2\gamma})$ (see \cite{Supplement}).
L000110	We use the late-time, trajectory-averaged squared overlap with the control state, $\bar\rho_{00} = \overline{\ev{\hat \rho(t\to\infty)}{0}}$ where $\hat\rho(t)$ is the density matrix, as an order parameter for control, which we can compute via the TWA under the full channel.
L000111	This formulation neglects interference and follows from the linear approximation in \cref{eq:control_channel}, so control-POVM nonlinearities are neglected as well.
L000112	
L000113	In \cref{fig:catmapfig}(a) we show $\bar{\rho}_{00}(p)$ for finite $\hbar$, where the transition is \emph{rounded} into a crossover but sharpens as $\hbar\rightarrow 0$ (i.e., $N\rightarrow\infty$).
L000114	Furthermore, we see this crossover smoothly connect the quantum data for $L\equiv\log_2N = 4$--$10$ to the TWA for $L=10$--$90$; the two agree quantitatively for $L=10$ ($N=1024$)~\cite{Supplement}.
L000115	The control strength $\theta$ is chosen so that $p_c=1/2$ as calculated by \cref{eq:pc}.
L000116	Scaling collapse near $p=p_c$ gives correlation length exponent $\nu\approx 1$, and examining $\bar{\rho}_{00}$ as a function of $L$ at $p_c$, shown \cref{fig:catmapfig}(b), gives the order parameter scaling exponent $\beta\approx 1$.
L000117	Figure \ref{fig:catmapfig}(c) shows $\bar\rho_{00}$ obtained with the TWA for $L=90$ for all $p$ and $\theta$, and clearly identifies the boundary between controlled and uncontrolled phases, agreeing with the expected classical result for $p_c$.
L000118	
L000119	The success of the TWA in capturing the behavior of the quantum cat map's control transition indicates that quantum interference effects are largely irrelevant, and suggests that the transition admits a universal description.
L000120	Motivated by this, we now isolate the dynamics near the unstable fixed point.
L000121	
L000122	\begin{figure}
L000123	    \centering
L000124	    \includegraphics[width=0.87\columnwidth]{quant+twa_full_plot.pdf}
L000125	    \caption{(a) Order parameter $\bar{\rho}_{00}(p)$ from exact cat-map simulations ($L=\log_2N=4$--$10$, blue) and TWA ($L=10$--$90$, red); inset: TWA collapse near $p=p_c$ with $\nu=1$ and $\beta=0.97$. (b) $\bar{\rho}_{00}(L)$ at $p_c$ for exact (blue) and TWA (red); dashed line: $\bar{\rho}_{00}\propto L^{-\beta}$ with $\beta=0.97$. (c) TWA $\bar{\rho}_{00}$ at $L=90$ vs $p,\theta$, consistent with the phase boundary $p_c(\theta)$ (white dash); $\hbar=\frac{1}{2\pi N}=\frac{1}{2^{L+1}\pi}$.}
L000126	    \label{fig:catmapfig}
L000127	\end{figure}
L000128	
L000129	
L000130	\textit{Inverted harmonic oscillator}---The saddle-point structure near an unstable fixed point is modeled by the inverted harmonic oscillator (IHO),
L000131	\begin{equation} \label{eq:IHOHam}
L000132	    \hat{H} = \frac{\hat{p}^2}{2} - \frac{\Omega^2\hat{x}^2}{2},
L000133	\end{equation}
L000134	where $\Omega$ controls the strength of the potential.
L000135	Time evolution under this Hamiltonian $\hat{U}(t) = \mathrm{exp}(-i\hat{H}t/\hbar)$ generates a single-mode squeezing operation and a Heisenberg-picture quantum channel
L000136	\begin{equation} \label{eq:UChannel}
L000137	    \mathbb{S}[\hat{\mathcal{O}}] = \hat{U}^\dagger(t)\,\hat{\mathcal{O}}\,\hat{U}(t).
L000138	\end{equation}
L000139	The action of this channel for time $t_S$ models the effect of a quantum-chaotic map near the fixed point with strength $\kappa = \Omega\, t_S$.
L000140	The operators $\hat{v}_\pm \equiv \sqrt{\Omega/2}\left(\hat{x}\pm\hat{p}/\Omega\right)$ grow or decay as $\mathbb{S}[\hat{v}_\pm] = e^{\pm \kappa} \hat{v}_\pm$, and all operators $(\hat{v}_+)^m(\hat{v}_-)^n$ are eigenvectors of the channel; $\expval{\hat{v}_\pm}$ are analogs of the components of the classical $\mathbf{r}$ above.
L000141	
L000142	As for the quantum cat map we can define ladder operators $\hat{a}$, $\hat{a}^\dagger$ and number states $\ket{n}$.
L000143	The vacuum $\ket{0}$ is the unstable fixed point of IHO evolution.
L000144	Control of the IHO can be implemented by introducing an ancilla mode with its own ladder operators $\hat{b},\hat{b}^\dagger$ and number states $\ket{n}_b$.
L000145	Initializing the ancilla in its vacuum $\ket{0}_b$, coupling it to the IHO, then measuring and resetting it back into the vacuum state yields Kraus operators $K_m(\theta) = \bra{m}_b e^{-i \theta(\hat{a}^\dagger \hat{b} + \hat{a}\hat{b}^\dagger)} \ket{0}_b$ which have exactly the same form as \cref{eq:ctrlKraus}.
L000146	The Heisenberg-picture quantum channel for control is
L000147	\begin{equation} \label{eq:KChannel}
L000148	    \mathbb{C}[\hat{\mathcal{O}}] = \sum_{m=0}^\infty \hat{K}_m^\dagger(\theta)\,\hat{\mathcal{O}}\,\hat{K}_m(\theta).
L000149	\end{equation}
L000150	All normally-ordered products of $\hat{a}$ and $\hat{a}^\dagger$ are eigenvectors of this channel, $\mathbb{C}[(\hat{a}^\dagger)^k \hat{a}^l] = \cos^{k+l}(\theta)\, (\hat{a}^\dagger)^k \hat{a}^l$.
L000151	As for \cref{eq:ctrlKraus}, $\mathbb{C}$ scales linear operators by $\cos\theta=e^{-\gamma}$.
L000152	
L000153	
L000154	The full stochastic evolution of a generic operator acting on the IHO is the weighted sum of these two channels
L000155	\begin{equation} \label{eq:fullChannel}
L000156	    \hat{\mathcal{O}}(t+1) = \mathbb{T}[\hat{\mathcal{O}}(t)] = (1-p)\mathbb{S}_\kappa[\hat{\mathcal{O}}(t)] + p\,\mathbb{C}_\gamma[\hat{\mathcal{O}}(t)],
L000157	\end{equation}
L000158	where channels are labeled by their respective exponential parameters.
L000159	
L000160	
L000161	\textit{Quantum dynamics and Gaussian state evolution}---The dynamics of $\expval{\hat{v}_\pm}$ under $\mathbb{S}_\kappa$ and $\mathbb{C}_\gamma$ follow the classical dynamics, and
L000162	quantum effects manifest only at higher orders in canonical operators.
L000163	Gaussian states are characterized by their mean $\expval{\hat{\mathbf{r}}}$ and covariance matrix,
L000164	\begin{equation}
L000165	    \sigma_{ij} = \frac{1}{2}\expval{\left\{\hat{r}_i, \hat{r}_j\right\}} - \expval{\hat{r}_i}\expval{\hat{r}_j}
L000166	\end{equation}
L000167	where $\hat{\mathbf{r}} = (\hat{v}_+ \quad \hat{v}_-)^T$ and $\{\,\cdot\,,\,\cdot\,\}$ is the anticommutator.
L000168	To highlight the quantum aspects of control we take $\expval{\hat{\mathbf{r}}} = 0$ so the system is classically controlled.
L000169	Because both $\mathbb{S}_\kappa$ and $\mathbb{C}_\gamma$ are Gaussian channels, Gaussian states remain Gaussian and we only need to track evolution of $\sigma$,
L000170	\begin{gather}
L000171	    \mathbb{S}_\kappa[\sigma] = \begin{pmatrix}
L000172	        e^{2\kappa}\sigma_{11} & \sigma_{12} \\
L000173	        \sigma_{12} & e^{-2\kappa}\sigma_{22}
L000174	    \end{pmatrix}\\
L000175	    \mathbb{C}_\gamma[\sigma] = e^{-2\gamma} \,\sigma + \frac{\hbar}{2}\left(1-e^{-2\gamma}\right) \,\mathbf{1}. \label{eq:control_channel}
L000176	\end{gather}
L000177	By design $\mathbb{S}_\kappa$ is a single-mode squeezing channel, and $\mathbb{C}_\gamma$ is a pure-loss attenuator~\cite{Serafini2021}.
L000178	For $p\neq0$ the off-diagonal elements of $\sigma$ vanish for $t\to\infty$ due to control, so $\sigma_{11},\sigma_{22}\equiv \sigma_\pm$ alone determine steady state properties.
L000179	These are the variances of canonical operators, so the constant term in $\mathbb{C}_\gamma$ manifests the quantum uncertainty relation between them, $\sigma_+\sigma_-\geq \hbar^2/4$, and the control point is $\sigma_\pm=\hbar/2$.
L000180	We note now that \cref{eq:control_channel} is the basis for the noise inserted via the TWA control protocol mentioned earlier.
L000181	The actions of $\mathbb{S}_\kappa$ and $\mathbb{C}_\gamma$ on $\sigma_\pm$ do not commute, complicating the analysis of trajectories of $\sigma_\pm$, however the system can be analyzed numerically together with analytical methods in applicable limits described later.
L000182	For convenience we scale $\sigma_\pm$ by $\hbar$ in our further analysis, leaving them dimensionless.
L000183	
L000184	
L000185	\textit{Semiclassical analysis of quantum control transition}---As above we use $\bar\rho_{00}$ as the order parameter, with the controlled state signaled by $\bar{\rho}_{00}>0$.
L000186	With our assumption $\expval{\hat{\mathbf{r}}}=0$, for a Gaussian trajectory $\rho_{00}(t) = \left[(\sigma_+(t)/+\tfrac{1}{2})(\sigma_-(t)+\tfrac{1}{2})\right]^{-1/2}$~\cite{Serafini2021}.
L000187	Since an arbitrary density matrix can be written as a linear combination of Gaussian density matrices, the order parameter $\bar{\rho}_{00}$ for a generic state is
L000188	\begin{equation} \label{eq:rho00}
L000189	    \bar{\rho}_{00} = \int\dd \sigma_+\dd\sigma_- \frac{\mathcal{Q}(\sigma_+,\sigma_-)}{\sqrt{(\sigma_++\frac{1}{2})(\sigma_-+\frac{1}{2})}},
L000190	\end{equation}
L000191	where $\mathcal{Q}(\sigma_+,\sigma_-)$ is the steady-state probability density over Gaussian trajectories which obeys
L000192	\begin{equation} \label{eq:Frobenius}
L000193	    \mathcal{Q}(\sigma_+,\sigma_-) = (1-p)\mathcal{Q}(\mathbb{S}_\kappa^{-1}[\sigma_+,\sigma_-]) + p\,\mathcal{Q}(\mathbb{C}_\gamma^{-1}[\sigma_+,\sigma_-]).
L000194	\end{equation}
L000195	This is analogous to the Frobenius-Perron equation for the invariant density used in classical chaotic dynamics~\cite{Hasegawa1992}
L000196	~\footnote{In classical systems, this operator is ill-defined for Hamiltonian dynamics, requiring the introduction of noise to regularize divergences~\cite{Supplement}.
L000197	In contrast, quantum fluctuations resolve these divergences.}.
L000198	We can compute \cref{eq:rho00} numerically.
L000199	
L000200	\begin{figure}[t]
L000201	    \centering
L000202	    \includegraphics[width=0.87\columnwidth]{full_plot.pdf}
L000203	    \caption{(a) Steady-state $\bar{\rho}_{00}(p)$ from the fixed point of $\mathbb{T}$ for $\kappa=\gamma=2\ln\!\left(\tfrac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2}\right)\approx0.42$, with cutoffs $2^{-L-1}<\sigma_\pm<2^{L-1}$ for $L=10,20,30,40,50$; dashed: late-time average over 5000 trajectories; inset: collapse near $p=p_c$ with $\nu=1$ and $\beta=0.96$. (b) $\bar{\rho}_{00}(L)$ at $p_c$ (same cutoffs); dashed: $\bar{\rho}_{00}\propto L^{-\beta}$ with $\beta=0.96$. (c) $\bar{\rho}_{00}(t)$ at $p_c$; dashed: $\bar{\rho}_{00}\propto t^{-1/z}$ with $z=2$.}
L000204	    \label{fig:rho00steady}
L000205	\end{figure}
L000206	
L000207	In \cref{fig:rho00steady} we show results computed with the IHO corresponding to those presented for the cat map in \cref{fig:catmapfig}.
L000208	The order parameter's dependence on $p$ shown in \cref{fig:rho00steady}(a) is computed both as an average over stochastic trajectories and as the fixed-point of the quantum channel after imposing cutoffs $\sigma_+ \in (1/2,2^{L-1})$ and $\sigma_- \in (2^{-L-1},1/2)$ \footnote{Due to normalization of $\sigma_\pm$ by $\hbar$, this cutoff defined by $L$ corresponds to taking $\hbar \sim 1/2^L$ similar to the cat map definition}.
L000209	The two methods agree for large $L$, with quick convergence for $p>p_c$ since values of $\sigma_\pm$ far from the control point are rarely explored.
L000210	Despite considering only classically-controlled states $\expval{\hat{\mathbf{r}}}=0$ we see a clear control transition sharpening at $p_c$ for large $L$.
L000211	Critical properties at $p_c$ are obtained from $L$ and $t$ dependence of $\bar{\rho}_{00}$, shown in \cref{fig:rho00steady}(b,c), and by collapsing the $p$-dependence of $\bar\rho_{00}$ near $p=p_c$.
L000212	We find $\beta \approx 1$ and $\nu\approx 1$, agreeing with the result of the cat map, and also dynamical exponent $z\approx2$, all characteristic of random walk universality.
L000213	
L000214	\textit{Fokker-Planck analysis}---Since $\sigma_- \in (0,1/2)$ at late times, $\bar{\rho}_{00}$ vanishing or remaining nonzero must result from $\sigma_+$ diverging for $p<p_c$ and remaining finite for $p>p_c$ in \cref{eq:rho00}.
L000215	The stochastic dynamics of $\sigma_+$ are a random multiplicative process with lower bound $\sigma_+>1/2$~\cite{Sornette1997}:
L000216	$\sigma_+(t+1)=\Lambda_t\, \sigma_+(t)$ where $\Lambda_t$ is drawn randomly from $(e^{2\kappa},e^{-2\gamma})$ with corresponding probabilities $(1-p,\, p)$, and $\sigma_+>1/2$ imposed by hand.
L000217	In the limit of weak dynamics ($\kappa,\gamma$ small) and defining $y=\ln\sigma_+$, we derive a Fokker-Planck (FP) equation for $P(y,t)$, the distribution of values taken by $y$ during these dynamics,
L000218	\begin{equation} \label{eq:FP0}
L000219	    \pdv{P(y,t)}{t} = -\bar{v}\pdv{P(y,t)}{y} + \mathcal{D}\pdv[2]{P(y,t)}{y},
L000220	\end{equation}
L000221	with drift velocity $\bar{v} = 2\kappa\left(p_c-p\right)/p_c$ and diffusion constant $\mathcal{D} = 2\kappa^2 p(1-p)/p_c^2$.
L000222	This can be solved for both $P(y,t)$ far from the boundary $y^\mathrm{min}=-\ln2$ and for the nontrivial steady state $P(y)$ for $p>p_c$,
L000223	\begin{gather}
L000224	    P(y,t) \sim \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi \mathcal{D}t}}e^{-\frac{\left(y-\bar{v}t\right)^2}{2\mathcal{D}t}},\,\, y\gg y^\mathrm{min} \\
L000225	    P(y) = \frac{\xi}{2^\xi} e^{-\xi y} \,\,\Rightarrow\,\,     \mathcal{Q}_+(\sigma_+) = \frac{\xi}{2^\xi}  \sigma_+^{-1-\xi},\,\, p>p_c, \label{eq:powerlawQ}
L000226	\end{gather}
L000227	where $\xi = \abs{\bar{v}}/\mathcal{D}$.
L000228	The distributions for $\sigma_+$ are obtained as $\mathcal{Q}_+(\sigma_+) = P(\ln\sigma_+)/\sigma_+$, and have log-normal and power-law forms respectively~\footnote{The distribution $\mathcal{Q}_+(\sigma_+)$ is related to the full distribution $\mathcal{Q}(\sigma_+,\sigma_-)$ appearing in \cref{eq:rho00} and \cref{eq:Frobenius} by integrating over the allowed values of $\sigma_-$, giving a distribution for the values of $\sigma_+$ alone.}.
L000229	In the Supplement~\cite{Supplement} we present the details of the FP derivation and demonstrate this log-normal behavior of $\mathcal{Q}_+(\sigma_+,t)$.
L000230	
L000231	With solutions for the distribution we can examine criticality of the transition.
L000232	At $p=p_c$, $\bar{v}=0$ so the distribution has no overall drift and $y$ evolves diffusively away from the boundary, yielding a divergence in $y$ (and $\sigma_+$) at late times,
L000233	\begin{equation} \label{eq:zeqn}
L000234	    \bar{\rho}_{00}(t) \sim \int_{\frac{1}{2}}^\infty\!\!\!\dd\sigma_+\frac{\mathcal{Q}_+(\sigma_+,t)}{\sqrt{\sigma_+(t)}} \sim \int_{0}^{\infty}\!\!\!\dd y\frac{e^{-\frac{y^2}{2\mathcal{D} t}}}{\sqrt{\mathcal{D}t}} e^{-\frac{y}{2}} \sim t^{-1/2}.
L000235	\end{equation}
L000236	If we also impose a maximum $\sigma_+^\mathrm{max} = 2^L$, giving $y^\mathrm{max}=L\ln2$, then for late times and large $L$ the hard wall boundaries combined with the diffusive nature of $P$ give $P(y)\sim1/L$ and we have
L000237	\begin{equation} \label{eq:betaeqn}
L000238	    \bar{\rho}_{00}(L) \sim \int_{\frac{1}{2}}^{2^L} \dd\sigma_+ \frac{\mathcal{Q}_+(\sigma_+)}{\sqrt{\sigma_+}}\sim\int_{0}^{L\ln2}\dd y \frac{e^{-\frac{y}{2}}}{L}\sim \frac{1}{L}.
L000239	\end{equation}
L000240	The Fokker-Planck analysis therefore recovers the critical behavior observed numerically in \cref{fig:rho00steady}(b,c) with exponents $\beta=1$ and $z=2$.
L000241	
L000242	The multiplicative dynamics used to obtain \cref{eq:FP0} are approximate.
L000243	The map $\mathbb{C}_\gamma$ imposes the constraint $\sigma_+>1/2$ in a smooth way, while the analysis here imposed it crudely, with the difference largest for $\sigma_+\sim1/2$.
L000244	However, the universal results \cref{eq:zeqn,eq:betaeqn} depend only on the large-$\sigma_+$ properties of \cref{eq:FP0}---diffusive behavior away from the boundary and normalizability of distributions---and critical properties are insensitive to precise behavior near $\sigma_+\sim 1/2$.
L000245	This is demonstrated in the Supplement~\cite{Supplement} by derivation of the FP equation from exact $\mathbb{S}_\kappa$ and $\mathbb{C}_\gamma$.
L000246	
L000247	\textit{Exact quantum dynamics}---The FP analysis developed above is semiclassical, but exact quantum results can be obtained as well.
L000248	Since $\hat{\mathbf{1}}$ and $\hat{v}_+$ are eigenvectors of $\mathbb{T}$ with eigenvalues $1$ and $(1-p)e^{\kappa}+p\,e^{-\gamma}$, an inductive argument can be developed using these base cases to give an infinite set of eigenvectors
L000249	\begin{equation}
L000250	    \hat{Z}_n = (\hat{v}_+)^n + \sum_{k=1}^{\lfloor n/2\rfloor} \alpha_{n,k} \hat{Z}_{n-2k} = \sum_{k=0}^{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}\beta_{n,k}(\hat{v}_+)^{n-2k},
L000251	\end{equation}
L000252	with eigenvalues $\lambda_n = (1-p)e^{n\kappa}+p\,e^{-n\gamma}$, i.e. $\mathbb{T}[\hat{Z}_n] = \lambda_n \hat{Z}_n$, where the $\lfloor n/2\rfloor$ coefficients $\alpha_{n,k}$ or $\beta_{n,k}$ are found by solving a system of linear equations.
L000253	The full construction is detailed in the Supplement~\cite{Supplement}.
L000254	
L000255	When $\lambda_n<1$ the operator $\hat{Z}_n$ is controlled, $\langle\hat{Z}_n\rangle\to0$ as $t\to\infty$.
L000256	Since the eigenvalues of all other order-$n$ eigenoperators are less than one at $p=p_c$, control of $\hat{Z}_n$ diagnoses controllability for all operators of order $n$.
L000257	We thus obtain
L000258	\begin{equation} \label{eq:pstar}
L000259	    p^\ast_n = \frac{e^{n\kappa}-1}{e^{n\kappa}-e^{-n\gamma}} >p_c\quad \forall n>0
L000260	\end{equation}
L000261	as the control probability for all order-$n$ operators, with $\lim_{n\to0}p^\ast_n = p_c$.
L000262	Since $p^\ast_{n+1}>p^\ast_n>p_c$ for $n>0$, as $p$ increases above $p_c$ there is a sequence of $p$ values where operators of increasing order become finite.
L000263	In the End Matter, we relate the sequence $p_n^\ast$ to moments of the steady-state distribution obtained from our Fokker-Planck analysis becoming finite.
L000264	
L000265	\textit{Conclusion}---We elucidate universal features of stochastic control transitions in quantum chaotic dynamics, using the quantum cat map as a concrete example.
L000266	Agreement between exact dynamics, TWA, and the controlled IHO shows that these universal features are set by uncertainty-limited fluctuations and are largely insensitive to quantum interference.
L000267	This framework explains the emergence of random-walk universality and how quantum fluctuations dress the controlled phase.
L000268	Previous work~\cite{Iadecola2023,LeMaire2024,Allocca2024,Pan2024,Pan2025} demonstrated stochastic quantum control \emph{without} full canonical quantization and hence lacked a formal connection to quantum chaos.
L000269	In this work, uncertainty-limited localization and the operator-moment hierarchy provide a genuinely quantum signature; this is underscored by the fact that the small-$\hbar$ physics is simulable with a classical control map with quantum-limited noise \cite{Supplement}.
L000270	Nonetheless, due to the power-law nature of the steady-state \cref{eq:powerlawQ}, there is potential for interference phenomenon to be seen in the nature of higher moments beyond the analysis here; we leave that analysis to future work.
L000271	
L000272	A direct platform for the IHO is a driven Kerr/parametric oscillator in cavity or circuit QED; above threshold the origin (vacuum) is an unstable fixed point and the dynamics form an effective phase-space double well while measurement-based feedback or autonomous reservoir engineering implements the attenuation control channel~\cite{Leghtas2015,Grimm2020,Sayrin2011,Vijay2012}.
L000273	Fully chaotic maps with clear unstable orbits have cold atomic candidates, where analogous control protocol could be designed~\cite{Moore1995,Chaudhury2009}.
L000274	
L000275	As noted above, the generic theory of the IHO cannot access the uncontrolled phase of specific maps.
L000276	Other quantum phenomena within that phase, such as qubit encoding, interference, and entanglement, can occur and are rich areas for future development.
L000277	Beyond these, a further question remains of how the \emph{many-body} problem behaves, where the phase space is extensive and Lyapunov exponents can undergo a transition themselves.
