L000556	\section{Fokker-Planck derivation}
L000557	
L000558	When the system is initialized in a state such that $\expval{\hat{x}}=\expval{\hat{p}}=0$, the late-time value of $\bar{\rho}_{00}=\overline{\mel{0}{\rho}{0}}$, which indicates whether the system is controlled, is determined by just a single element of the covariance matrix, $\sigma_+ = \expval{(\hat{v}_+)^2}$.
L000559	With the known form of the channels $\mathbb{S}_\kappa$ and $\mathbb{C}_\gamma$, the evolution of $\sigma_+$ can be expressed as a modified multiplicative process:
L000560	\begin{equation}
L000561	    \sigma_+(t+\delta t) = e^{f(\sigma_+,v)}e^{v\,\delta t}\,\sigma_+(t),
L000562	\end{equation}
L000563	where $v$ is drawn randomly from the distribution
L000564	\begin{equation}
L000565	    \pi(v) = (1-p)\,\delta(v-2\Omega)+p\,\delta(v+2\Gamma)
L000566	\end{equation}
L000567	and
L000568	\begin{equation}
L000569	    f(\sigma_+,v) = \log\left(1+h(v)\frac{e^{-v\,\delta t}-1}{2 \,\sigma_+}\right),
L000570	\end{equation}
L000571	with $h(2\Omega)=0$ and $h(-2\Gamma)=1$; the form of $h(v)$ is not yet determined beyond the values it takes at these two points.
L000572	The factor $e^{f(\sigma_+,v)}$ comes from the term in the control evolution imposing the lower bound on $\sigma_+$ from the uncertainty relation.
L000573	The parameters we use for the strength of the quantum channels are $\kappa = \Omega\delta t$ and $\gamma = \Gamma\delta t$.
L000574	The time step length $\delta t$ is superfluous in principle; only the strengths of the maps $\kappa$ and $\gamma$ are relevant for the dynamics.
L000575	Including this $\delta t$, however, allows us to more easily take a continuous time limit later in the derivation while avoiding pathological behavior of the yet-undetermined function $h(v)$.
L000576	
L000577	Putting $\sigma_+ = \frac{1}{2}e^{x}$ we can rewrite the dynamics in terms of a modified random walk of the exponential variable $x$~\cite{Sornette1997}.
L000578	The factor of $\frac{1}{2}$ is chosen so that the control point $\sigma_+ = \frac{1}{2}$ corresponds to $x=0$.
L000579	The time evolution of $x$ is then
L000580	\begin{equation}
L000581	    x(t + \delta t) = T(x(t),v) = x(t) + v \,\delta t+ \tilde{f}(x(t),v),
L000582	\end{equation}
L000583	where $\tilde{f}(x,v) = f(\tfrac{1}{2}e^x,v)$.
L000584	The preimage of $x(t)$ for a given $v$ is
L000585	\begin{equation}
L000586	    T^{-1}(x,v) = x - v\,\delta t + \tilde{g}\left(x,v\right) \quad \text{with}\quad \tilde{g}(x,v) = \log\left[1-h(v)\left(1-e^{v\,\delta t}\right)e^{-x}\right].
L000587	\end{equation}
L000588	Letting $\mathcal{P}(x,t)$ be the distribution of values $x$ takes after many time steps, we have
L000589	\begin{align}
L000590	    \mathcal{P}&(x, t+\delta t) = \int_{-\infty}^\infty\dd v\,\pi(v)\,\mathcal{P}\left(T^{-1}(x,v),t\right) = \int_{-\infty}^\infty\dd v\,\pi(v)\,\mathcal{P}\left(x-v\,\delta t+\tilde{g}(x,v),t\right)\\
L000591	    &= \int_{-\infty}^\infty\dd (\delta v)\,\pi(\bar{v}+\delta v)\,\mathcal{P}\left(x-\bar{v}\,\delta t-\delta v\,\delta t+\tilde{g}(x,\bar{v}+\delta v),t\right) \\
L000592	    &\approx \mathcal{P}(T^{-1}(x,\bar{v}),t) + \delta t^2\frac{\expval{\delta v^2}}{2} \Bigg[\frac{1}{\delta t^2}\eval{\pdv[2]{\tilde{g}(x,v)}{v}}_{v=\bar{v}} \pdv{\mathcal{P}(y,t)}{y} + \left(1-\frac{1}{\delta t}\eval{\pdv{\tilde{g}(x,v)}{v}}_{v=\bar{v}}\right)^2 \pdv[2]{\mathcal{P}(y,t)}{y}\Bigg]_{y=T^{-1}(x,\bar{v})}, \label{eq:discreteFP}
L000593	\end{align}
L000594	where in the second line we put $v=\bar{v}+\delta v$ with $\bar{v} = \int\dd v\,v\,\pi(v) = 2\Omega-2p(\Gamma+\Omega)$ the average value of $v$, so that $\int\dd v\,\delta v\,\pi(v) = 0$.
L000595	In the final line we expand in $\delta v$, which involves expanding both the distribution $P$ and the function $\tilde{g}$.
L000596	This assumes that $\pi(v)$ is relatively sharply peaked around $\bar{v}$.
L000597	We also pull a factor of $\delta t^2$ from the entire bracketed expression.
L000598	Using one of these factors of $\delta t$, the expectation value of $\delta v^2$ can be manipulated to give a diffusion constant,
L000599	\begin{align}
L000600	    \mathcal{D} &\equiv \delta t\frac{\expval{\delta v^2}}{2} = \frac{\delta t}{2}\int_{-\infty}^\infty\dd v \,\pi(v)\,(v-\bar{v})^2 \\
L000601	    &= \frac{\delta t}{2}\left[4p^2(1-p)(\Gamma+\Omega)^2+4p(1-p)^2(\Gamma+\Omega)^2\right] = 2p(1-p)(\Gamma+\Omega)^2\delta t.
L000602	\end{align}
L000603	If we define a step size $\ell = v\delta t$ then we see that the diffusion constant is the mean-square step size per time step.
L000604	It grows with the difference between the two possible values of $v$---if they are very different, then trajectories will diverge quickly, i.e. fast diffusion.
L000605	
L000606	Now consider a limit such that our dynamics are well-approximated as continuous in time.
L000607	We can accomplish this by considering $\delta t$ small with fixed $\Omega,\Gamma$, but we should think of this as the limit of weak maps, i.e. small $\kappa,\gamma$.
L000608	Expanding in $\delta t$ helps us avoid issues with the yet-undetermined function $h(v)$, but in the end the value of $\delta t$ is set to 1.
L000609	
L000610	Returning to \cref{eq:discreteFP}, we bring $P(T^{-1}(x,\bar{v}),t)$ to the left-hand side and divide both sides by $\delta t$ giving
L000611	\begin{equation}
L000612	    \frac{1}{\delta t}\left[\mathcal{P}(x,t+\delta t)-\mathcal{P}(T^{-1}(x,\bar{v}),t)\right]
L000613	    \underset{\delta t \text{ small}}{\longrightarrow} \pdv{\mathcal{P}(x,t)}{t} + \bar{v}\left(1-h(\bar{v})e^{-x}\right)\pdv{\mathcal{P}(x,t)}{x},
L000614	\end{equation}
L000615	on the left-hand side, where we have expanded $T^{-1}(x,\bar{v}) = x - \bar{v}\,\delta t + \tilde{g}(x,\bar{v})$ using $\tilde{g}(x,v)\approx\delta t\,v\,h(v)\,e^{-x}+\delta t^2\frac{v^2}{2}(1-h(v)e^{-x})h(v)e^{-x}$.
L000616	This could alternatively be written as a total time derivative with $x(t)$ evolving ballistically with velocity $\bar{v}$.
L000617	
L000618	The right-hand side of \cref{eq:discreteFP} has factors related to the derivatives of $\tilde{g}(x,v)$,
L000619	\begin{gather}
L000620	    \frac{1}{\delta t}\eval{\pdv{\tilde{g}(x,v)}{v}}_{v=\bar{v}} = \eval{\pdv{\left(v\,h(v)\right)}{v}}_{v=\bar{v}}\,e^{-x} + O(\delta t) \\
L000621	    \frac{1}{\delta t^2}\eval{\pdv[2]{\tilde{g}(x,v)}{v}}_{v=\bar{v}} = \frac{1}{\delta t}\eval{\pdv[2]{\left(v\,h(v)\right)}{v}}_{v=\bar{v}}e^{-x} +
L000622	    \frac{1}{2}\eval{\pdv[2]{\left[v^2(1-h(v)e^{-x})h(v)\right]}{v}}_{v=\bar{v}}e^{-x} + O(\delta t). \label{eq:d2gdv2}
L000623	\end{gather}
L000624	The $1/\delta t$ in the first term of \cref{eq:d2gdv2} presents a problem.
L000625	Though we need to expand the derivatives of $\mathcal{P}$ in powers of $\delta t$ since they are evaluated at $T^{-1}(x,\bar{v})$ and not $x$, when the $1/\delta t$ term of \cref{eq:d2gdv2} multiplies $\partial \mathcal{P}(x,t)/\partial x$ there is no factor of $\delta t$ to cancel it, leading to a divergence for small $\delta t$ unless we impose a condition to cancel it in general,
L000626	\begin{equation}
L000627	    \pdv[2]{\left(v\,h(v)\right)}{v} = 0 \quad \Rightarrow \quad h(v) = \frac{\Gamma}{v}\frac{v-2\Omega}{\Gamma+\Omega}.
L000628	\end{equation}
L000629	Since $h(v)$ was not determined when introduced besides its value at $v=2\Omega$ and $v=-2\Gamma$ we are free to use this form to satisfy this additional constraint and ensure a well-formed Fokker-Planck equation in the continuous time limit.
L000630	As a result of this condition, no additional terms involving additional derivatives of $P$ will appear.
L000631	The needed derivatives of $\tilde{g}$ are
L000632	\begin{align}
L000633	    &\eval{\pdv{(v\,h(v))}{v}}_{v=\bar{v}} = \frac{\Gamma}{\Gamma+\Omega} = \frac{\gamma}{\kappa+\gamma} = 1-p_c \equiv \lambda \\
L000634	    &\frac{1}{2}\eval{\pdv[2]{\left[v^2(1-h(v)e^{-x})h(v)\right]}{v}}_{v=\bar{v}}e^{-x} \nonumber \\
L000635	    &\quad= \left(1-\frac{\Gamma}{\Gamma+\Omega}e^{-x}\right)\frac{\Gamma}{\Gamma+\Omega}e^{-x} = \left(1-\lambda e^{-x}\right)\lambda e^{-x}
L000636	\end{align}
L000637	
L000638	We therefore have the Fokker-Planck equation
L000639	\begin{equation} \label{eq:FokkerPlanck1}
L000640	    \pdv{\mathcal{P}(x,t)}{t} = -v(x)\pdv{\mathcal{P}(x,t)}{x} + \mathcal{D}\,r(x) \pdv{}{x}\left[r(x)\pdv{\mathcal{P}(x,t)}{x}\right],
L000641	\end{equation}
L000642	with
L000643	\begin{align}
L000644	    r(x) &= 1-\lambda e^{-x}\\
L000645	    v(x) &= \bar{v}\left(1-\,h(\bar{v})e^{-x}\right) = \left(\bar{v}+ 2\kappa \frac{\lambda}{e^x-\lambda}\right)r(x).
L000646	\end{align}
L000647	Notice that everywhere we have a spatial derivative we have a factor of $r(x)$, suggesting that we can change coordinates to make \cref{eq:FokkerPlanck1} take a cleaner form.
L000648	Putting
L000649	\begin{equation}
L000650	    z \equiv \log\left(e^x-\lambda\right) \,\Rightarrow \pdv{}{z} = r(x)\pdv{}{x},
L000651	\end{equation}
L000652	and defining $P(z,t) = \mathcal{P}\left(x(z),t\right)$ we obtain
L000653	\begin{align} \label{eq:FokkerPlanck2}
L000654	    \pdv{P(z,t)}{t} &= -(\bar{v}+2\gamma\,p_c e^{-z})\pdv{P(z,t)}{z} + \mathcal{D}\pdv[2]{P(z,t)}{z} \\
L000655	    &= -2\gamma\, p_c e^{-z} P(z,t)-\pdv{\left[\left(\bar{v}+2\gamma\, p_c e^{-z}\right)P(z,t)\right]}{z} + \mathcal{D}\pdv[2]{P(z,t)}{z}.
L000656	\end{align}
L000657	As a result, the original element of the covariance matrix is $\sigma_+ = \frac{1}{2}e^x = \frac{1}{2}(e^z-p_c+1)$, with the control point $\sigma_+=\frac{1}{2}$ now equating to $z = \log(p_c)$.
L000658	
L000659	The Fokker-Planck equation obtained here has followed from the exact stochastic dynamics on $\sigma_+$, and we see that the equation analyzed in the main text,
L000660	\begin{equation}
L000661	    \pdv{P(z,t)}{t} = -\bar{v}\pdv{P(z,t)}{z} + \mathcal{D}\pdv[2]{P(z,t)}{z},
L000662	\end{equation}
L000663	along with constraint $z>-\ln(2)$, agrees with this exact equation in the large-$z$ limit.
L000664	Accordingly, the solutions for the distribution $P(z,t)$ and therefore $\mathcal{Q}_+(\sigma_+)$ far from this boundary are also valid, power-law at $p=p_c$ and log-normal for early times and $p\neq p_c$.
L000665	The power-law critical behavior is discussed in the main text, and in \cref{fig:lognormal} we also show agreement with the log-normal distribution in the appropriate limits.
L000666	
L000667	\begin{figure}[b]
L000668	    \centering
L000669	    \includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{lognormal_plots.pdf}
L000670	    \caption{The early-time distribution of values taken by $\sigma_+$ in numerical simulations with $p=0.4<p_c$ compared to the predicted log-normal form of $\mathcal{Q}_\sigma(\sigma_+)$, shown in a log-log plot.
L000671	    The simulations initialize $\sigma_+(0)=1/2$ and show the values of $\sigma_+(t_f)$ across $5 \times 10^6$ stochastic trajectories.
L000672	    For $\kappa=\gamma=0.02$ we take $t_f=25$, and for $\kappa=\gamma=0.2$ we take $t_f=100$.}
L000673	    \label{fig:lognormal}
